1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:01,621
HeatFlood Sentinel.

2
00:00:01,671 --> 00:00:05,918
A scalable big data and deep learning
framework for heatwave and flash flood

3
00:00:05,968 --> 00:00:08,809
forecasting and detection in Pakistan.

4
00:00:08,859 --> 00:00:13,770
An M.S. Data Science thesis at NED
University of Engineering and Technology,

5
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:18,230
by Aseef Ahmed, supervised by Doctor Maria
Andaleeb Siddiqui.

6
00:00:18,851 --> 00:00:24,113
Pakistan faces two linked climate hazards:
extreme heat before the monsoon,

7
00:00:24,163 --> 00:00:25,917
and sudden flooding during it.

8
00:00:25,986 --> 00:00:31,678
HeatFlood Sentinel ingests eight years of
ERA5 reanalysis for twenty Pakistani

9
00:00:31,728 --> 00:00:36,834
cities, one point four million hourly
rows, and benchmarks statistical,

10
00:00:36,884 --> 00:00:41,926
machine learning and deep learning models
on a fully held-out 2022 test year.

11
00:00:41,976 --> 00:00:45,728
The home page shows headline accuracy, the
hazard map,

12
00:00:45,778 --> 00:00:49,329
and an alert replay from the streamed 2022
season.

13
00:00:49,951 --> 00:00:54,614
The Live Alerts page runs the same models
on real-time Open-Meteo data.

14
00:00:54,664 --> 00:01:00,174
Every six hours, a GitHub Actions workflow
pulls fresh observations and forecasts,

15
00:01:00,224 --> 00:01:03,937
streams them through the alert engine, and
republishes the map,

16
00:01:03,987 --> 00:01:06,926
the open alerts, and this all-cities
table.

17
00:01:07,537 --> 00:01:10,086
Each of the twenty cities has its own
page.

18
00:01:10,155 --> 00:01:14,766
Here is Karachi, then Jacobabad: a
seventy-two hour outlook,

19
00:01:14,816 --> 00:01:20,665
live hazard checks, daily forecasts
against ERA5 for the 2022 test year,

20
00:01:20,715 --> 00:01:24,682
and the model's heatwave and flash flood
probabilities through the season.

21
00:01:25,310 --> 00:01:30,312
For forecasting, the hourly LSTM predicts
temperature one hour ahead with an error

22
00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:35,026
of just zero point six three four degrees,
against one point five six for

23
00:01:35,076 --> 00:01:36,145
persistence.

24
00:01:36,195 --> 00:01:39,994
At six hours it still holds one point
three seven degrees.

25
00:01:40,604 --> 00:01:42,853
Detection is reported honestly.

26
00:01:42,987 --> 00:01:48,562
For flash floods six hours ahead, XGBoost
reaches a critical success index of zero

27
00:01:48,612 --> 00:01:52,124
point six zero nine, ahead of the
persistence rule.

28
00:01:52,232 --> 00:01:57,052
For heatwaves one day ahead, however,
simple persistence still wins,

29
00:01:57,102 --> 00:02:02,468
with a CSI of zero point seven one seven
against zero point three five six for

30
00:02:02,518 --> 00:02:03,673
XGBoost.

31
00:02:03,729 --> 00:02:07,976
Heat events last for days, so yesterday is
a strong predictor,

32
00:02:08,026 --> 00:02:10,262
and the thesis says so plainly.

33
00:02:10,884 --> 00:02:16,003
The benchmark page compares LSTM,
ConvLSTM, XGBoost,

34
00:02:16,053 --> 00:02:21,850
SARIMA, persistence and climatology, with
Diebold-Mariano significance tests.

35
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:27,826
On the gridded two-degree ERA5 lattice,
the ConvLSTM beats persistence at every

36
00:02:27,876 --> 00:02:32,221
lead from one to six hours, for both
temperature and rainfall.

37
00:02:32,804 --> 00:02:36,629
This thesis figure shows that gap widening
with lead time:

38
00:02:36,684 --> 00:02:41,634
at six hours the ConvLSTM's temperature
error is two point two nine degrees,

39
00:02:41,684 --> 00:02:44,545
against six point one seven for
persistence.

40
00:02:45,137 --> 00:02:48,389
Explainability shows why a model raised an
alert.

41
00:02:48,445 --> 00:02:52,835
For the heatwave detector, the strongest
driver is the daily maximum temperature

42
00:02:52,885 --> 00:02:57,275
anomaly, followed by exceedance of the
ninetieth and ninety-fifth percentile

43
00:02:57,325 --> 00:02:58,473
thresholds.

44
00:02:58,523 --> 00:03:02,334
Saliency maps do the same for the
ConvLSTM.

45
00:03:02,951 --> 00:03:07,114
The streaming pipeline replayed the full
2022 season:

46
00:03:07,182 --> 00:03:11,182
eighty-seven thousand eight hundred and
forty events at two hundred and ten point

47
00:03:11,232 --> 00:03:12,731
five events per second.

48
00:03:12,781 --> 00:03:18,434
Under chaos tests, duplicates, corrupt
records, late data and outages never

49
00:03:18,484 --> 00:03:23,239
crashed it, and on a kill and restart it
lost zero alerts and produced zero

50
00:03:23,289 --> 00:03:24,398
duplicates.

51
00:03:24,448 --> 00:03:28,031
The same pipeline now also runs on a
single-node Hadoop,

52
00:03:28,081 --> 00:03:33,421
YARN, Spark and Kafka cluster: about two
thousand seven hundred events per second

53
00:03:33,471 --> 00:03:39,033
on a backlog, and a kill and restart test
with zero lost and zero duplicate events

54
00:03:39,083 --> 00:03:40,179
in the output.

55
00:03:40,229 --> 00:03:43,116
The numbers on this page come from the
local runtime.

56
00:03:43,751 --> 00:03:47,542
Batch processing scales almost linearly
with data volume,

57
00:03:47,592 --> 00:03:51,070
and two workers gave a one point eight
eight times speed-up,

58
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:53,187
at ninety-four percent efficiency.

59
00:03:53,784 --> 00:03:58,460
The architecture follows a medallion data
lake, from bronze raw data to silver

60
00:03:58,510 --> 00:04:03,382
cleaned data to gold features, feeding the
models, the stream processor,

61
00:04:03,432 --> 00:04:07,374
and a FastAPI service that is exported to
this static site.

62
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:13,657
The case study replays Jacobabad in 2022,
one of the hottest cities on Earth,

63
00:04:13,707 --> 00:04:17,967
through its pre-monsoon heatwave and the
monsoon floods that followed,

64
00:04:18,017 --> 00:04:21,737
showing exactly when the system would have
raised each alert.

65
00:04:22,364 --> 00:04:25,550
Here is the same replay as it appears in
the thesis.

66
00:04:26,249 --> 00:04:30,939
Finally, the research page lists the
thesis and two review manuscripts in

67
00:04:30,989 --> 00:04:35,873
preparation, the API docs describe every
endpoint behind the dashboard,

68
00:04:35,923 --> 00:04:39,579
and the about page explains how the
project is kept up to date.

69
00:04:40,171 --> 00:04:45,433
All of the code is open source on GitHub,
released as version zero point one point

70
00:04:45,483 --> 00:04:50,454
zero, with fifty-eight automated tests
passing in continuous integration.

71
00:04:51,130 --> 00:04:52,291
Thank you for watching.

72
00:04:52,341 --> 00:04:56,244
Explore the live dashboard and the code at
the links on screen.

